$\begin{aligned}2\sin(2x)+4&=1\\2\sin(2x)&=-3\\\sin(2x)&=-\frac32\end{aligned}$

Sine lies between $-1$ and $1$, so it cannot equal $-\frac32$. There are no solutions.

The graph confirms this: $y=2\sin(2x)+4$ has minimum value $2$, above the line $y=1$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{linered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=4.8cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black},tick style={black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},xmin=-0.2,xmax=6.6,ymin=0,ymax=6.6,xtick={0,1.570796327,3.141592654,4.712388980,6.283185307},xticklabels={$0$,$\frac\pi2$,$\pi$,$\frac{3\pi}2$,$2\pi$},ytick={1,2,4,6},samples=160,clip=true]
\addplot[curveblue,thick,domain=0:6.283185307] {2*sin(deg(2*x))+4};
\addplot[linered,thick,dashed,domain=0:6.283185307] {1};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Blue curve: $y=2\sin(2x)+4$. Red dashed line: $y=1$.

$\begin{aligned}4\cos(2x)-3&=1\\4\cos(2x)&=4\\\cos(2x)&=1\end{aligned}$

Let $u=2x$, so $0\le u\le4\pi$.

Cosine equals $1$ at the start and end of each full revolution. Include both endpoints of the domain.

$u=0,\ 2\pi,\ 4\pi$.

$\begin{aligned}x&=\frac u2\\x&=0,\ \pi,\ 2\pi\end{aligned}$

The line $y=1$ meets the curve at its three maxima in the domain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{linered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=4.8cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black},tick style={black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},xmin=-0.2,xmax=6.6,ymin=-7.6,ymax=1.8,xtick={0,1.570796327,3.141592654,4.712388980,6.283185307},xticklabels={$0$,$\frac\pi2$,$\pi$,$\frac{3\pi}2$,$2\pi$},ytick={-7,-3,1},samples=160,clip=true]
\addplot[curveblue,thick,domain=0:6.283185307] {4*cos(deg(2*x))-3};
\addplot[linered,thick,dashed,domain=0:6.283185307] {1};
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,mark size=1.6pt,black] coordinates {(0,1) (3.141592654,1) (6.283185307,1)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Blue curve: $y=4\cos(2x)-3$. Red dashed line: $y=1$. Black points mark the solutions.

